USAGE: oss [-h] [-n #] [-s #] [-t #] [-i] [-f]
[-h]: Throws help message: No execution.\n");
[-n]: expects integer value for max total children to launch
[-s]: expects integer value for number of max simultaneous children
[-t]: expects integer value for secons of hang time
[-i]: expects integer value for child interval launch
[-f]: expects string for log file, defaults to log.txt

EXAMPLEs:
./oss -n 12 -s 3 -t 4
./oss -s 5 
./oss -n 7 -t 4
./oss -h
./oss -n 3 -s 2 -t 7 -i 300 -f newlog.txt

COMPILE:

	Compile using make utiliy within directory containing oss.c, 
	user.c, and makefile.

RUN:

	After compiling, run program using './oss' execution with 
	appropriate usage as above.

DESCRIPTION:

	OSS program will create a number of child processes, up to S simultaneous children,
	until it has created N total children. 

		oss will message these processes to pass data about the queue state and time usage.

	The child process, USER, will iterate through a loop 
		
		during each loop of the worker process a random chance asking OSS for a resource or a premature termination can occur
		Oss wil handle these conditions and insert and remove from blocked queue or allocate resources

	Upon USER closing, OSS will continue to launch a number of
	chilren, up to S simultaneous, until N children is reached.

	Children will be created at I interval.

	Log file will be save to F.

OUTSTANDING ISSUES:

	None immediately aware of. Seems to run fine. Log file cleaner then output to screen. 

DEVELOPEMENT ISSUES:

	One major hurdle i faced was the way i implemented which resource children would request.
	I eventually had to go back and rework my logic to work with a pointer array to the resource arrays
	this allowed much a more clever way to handle the data from messages in one go rather than with a crazy switch statement.
	I also had issues where when I forced a kill on a deadlock, the wait check at the top of the loop would decrement values redundantly
	once this was found it was ,uch easier to see correct behavior in resource and process tables
	this double decrement would also kill the program prematurely but again fixed

	
DEADLOCK DETECTION:

	When we check for dead locks, we will loop through the pcb and see if ALL current processes are blocked.
	then we check for an easy unblock and message the child if we can clear it.
	but if we cant clear the condition of all deadlocks after trying this we need to make a sacrifice
	kill the first process we find in the pcb and release its resources
	loop around and let children deal with the freed resources.   
	I cannot claim this is elegant but it seems to work after refining the behavior.

CHILD BEHAVIOR:

	Children have a 60% chance to request a resource, a 15% to release an owned resource, and 5% chance to terminate, every 250ms.
